{
  "concept": "A rational function can break in two fundamentally different ways: a removable discontinuity, where a single point is missing from an otherwise smooth curve, and a vertical asymptote, where the function grows without bound on both sides.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Coordinate plane with dotted gray grid spanning x from about -4 to 10 and y from -7 to 7",
    "Horizontal x-axis with arrowhead and label 'x', tick labels at -3, -1, 1, 3, 5, 7, 9",
    "Vertical y-axis with arrowhead and label 'y', tick labels at -6, -4, -2, 2, 4, 6",
    "Teal left branch: enters from far left nearly flat just above the x-axis, crosses the x-axis at x = 1, then plunges steeply downward off the bottom of the frame as x approaches 3",
    "Small hollow open circle (teal outline, white fill) on the left branch at x = -1 marking the removed point",
    "Teal right branch: descends from high above near x just greater than 3, falls steeply, then levels off approaching y = 1 at the right edge",
    "Red dashed vertical line at x = 3 spanning the full plot height",
    "Red label below the axis: 'Vertical asymptote at x = 3'",
    "Blue annotation at upper left: 'Removable discontinuity at x = -1' with a thin arrow pointing to the open circle",
    "Movable trace point marker on the curve with a readout of (x, y)",
    "Optional dashed horizontal line at y = 1 indicating the end behavior of the right branch",
    "Formula caption showing the function in factored and cancelled forms"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "breakType",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the hole and the dashed line are two different kinds of failure: one removes a single point, the other destroys the function's values near a whole line.",
      "label": "Highlight break",
      "options": [
        "Removable discontinuity (x = -1)",
        "Vertical asymptote (x = 3)",
        "Both"
      ],
      "default": "Both"
    },
    {
      "id": "traceX",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That approaching x = 3 from either side drives the function value to negative or positive infinity, so no single value can patch the break.",
      "label": "Trace point at x =",
      "range": [
        -3.5,
        9.5,
        0.05
      ],
      "default": 5
    },
    {
      "id": "factorView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the hole comes from a factor that cancels in the algebra — the simplified function is perfectly smooth at x = -1, and only the original's domain excludes that one point.",
      "label": "Algebraic form",
      "options": [
        "Original (factored)",
        "After cancelling (x + 1)"
      ],
      "default": "Original (factored)"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The whole graph",
      "narration": "A single rational function drawn in two teal branches. Two things interrupt it: a tiny hollow circle at x = -1 and a red dashed line at x = 3.",
      "state": {
        "breakType": "Both",
        "traceX": 5,
        "factorView": "Original (factored)"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "A single missing point",
      "narration": "Highlighting the removable discontinuity shows the curve arriving at x = -1 from both sides at the same height — only the one point is punched out.",
      "state": {
        "breakType": "Removable discontinuity (x = -1)",
        "traceX": -1.4,
        "factorView": "Original (factored)"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why it is removable",
      "narration": "Cancelling the shared (x + 1) factor gives a function that is perfectly continuous there. Redefining one value would repair the graph entirely.",
      "state": {
        "breakType": "Removable discontinuity (x = -1)",
        "traceX": -1,
        "factorView": "After cancelling (x + 1)"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Approaching x = 3 from the left",
      "narration": "Now drag the trace point toward 3 from the left. The y-value dives past -6 and keeps falling — there is no height to land on.",
      "state": {
        "breakType": "Vertical asymptote (x = 3)",
        "traceX": 2.9,
        "factorView": "Original (factored)"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Approaching x = 3 from the right",
      "narration": "From the right the value rockets upward instead. Opposite infinities on the two sides mean this break can never be patched by a single point.",
      "state": {
        "breakType": "Vertical asymptote (x = 3)",
        "traceX": 3.1,
        "factorView": "Original (factored)"
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two breaks, two causes",
      "narration": "The hole comes from a factor that cancels; the asymptote comes from a factor that survives in the denominator. Same graph, two very different failures.",
      "state": {
        "breakType": "Both",
        "traceX": 5,
        "factorView": "Original (factored)"
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A denominator factor that cancels leaves only a hole you could fill with one value; a factor that does not cancel sends the function to infinity, and no single value can ever repair it.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Textbook-style coordinate plane on white with a light dotted gray grid, axes in black with arrowheads and italic x and y labels; odd-numbered ticks on x (-3 to 9), even-numbered ticks on y (-6 to 6). The function curve is drawn in a medium teal, fairly thick, in two branches: the left branch comes in from the far left just above y = 0, stays nearly flat, crosses the x-axis at x = 1, then bends sharply downward and exits the bottom of the frame just left of x = 3; the right branch enters the top of the frame just right of x = 3, drops steeply, and flattens toward y = 1 by x = 9. A white-filled teal-outlined open circle sits on the left branch at x = -1. A bold red dashed vertical line sits at x = 3 with the red caption 'Vertical asymptote at x = 3' placed below the x-axis to the right. A blue multi-line annotation 'Removable discontinuity at x = -1' sits in the upper-left margin with a thin black arrow to the open circle. When a break is highlighted, that marker brightens and thickens while the other dims to gray. The trace point is a filled dot on the curve with a small coordinate readout that turns red and reads large magnitudes as x nears 3. In the cancelled view, show the simplified formula and a faint ghost of the filled-in point at x = -1 while keeping the open circle visible."
}

